{"id":557578210,"iid":266075,"project_id":278964,"title":"Allow tableColumnOrder in work item display settings","description":"## What does this MR do and why?\n\nBackend part of the table view column menu. The frontend is in https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/gitlab/-/merge_requests/265645, which depends on this MR.\n\n- Adds an optional `tableColumnOrder` property to the work item display settings JSON schema. It's an ordered list of table column keys.\n- The schema has `additionalProperties: false`, so the frontend can't save the column order until this property exists.\n- It's stored in the existing `work_item_type_user_preferences.display_settings` jsonb column (per user and namespace) and in saved views. No migration is needed.\n- It uses the same limits as `groupOrder`, `visibleGroups` and `collapsedGroups`: up to 100 unique strings of up to 256 characters each.\n- If the property is missing, the table uses its default column order.\n\n## References\n\n- https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/gitlab/-/work_items/634154\n- Frontend: https://gitlab.com/gitlab-org/gitlab/-/merge_requests/265645\n\n## Screenshots or screen recordings\n\nNot applicable, backend only.\n\n## How to set up and validate locally\n\n1. Run the model spec:\n   ```shell\n   bin/rspec spec/models/work_items/user_preference_spec.rb -e 'tableColumnOrder'\n   ```\n1. Or, in the Rails console, check that a preference with `display_settings: { 'tableColumnOrder' =\u003e %w[status assignee] }` is valid, and that duplicate or non-string entries are rejected.\n\n## MR acceptance checklist\n\nEvaluate this MR against the [MR acceptance checklist](https://docs.gitlab.com/development/code_review/#acceptance-checklist).\nIt helps you analyze changes to reduce risks in quality, performance, reliability, security, and maintainability.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","state":"opened","created_at":"2026-10-28T23:35:37.990Z","updated_at":"2026-10-28T23:51:48.984Z","merged_by":null,"mer